### What triggers a cascading failure?

The anatomy begins with a single dependency getting slow, not even down, just slow. One service calls it synchronously and waits. Because the caller waits longer, its own threads stay busy longer, so its thread pool fills. Now the caller is slow too. Its callers wait, their pools fill, and the slowness climbs up the chain.

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

public class CascadeTrigger {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("1. Fraud-check dependency gets slow (500ms → 5000ms)");
        System.out.println("2. Transfer service threads wait on fraud-check");
        System.out.println("3. Transfer service thread pool fills (all threads waiting)");
        System.out.println("4. Transfer service is now slow too");
        System.out.println("5. Gateway threads wait on transfer service");
        System.out.println("6. Gateway thread pool fills");
        System.out.println("7. ENTIRE SYSTEM frozen from one slow dependency");
    }
}
```

```text
1. Fraud-check dependency gets slow (500ms → 5000ms)
2. Transfer service threads wait on fraud-check
3. Transfer service thread pool fills (all threads waiting)
4. Transfer service is now slow too
5. Gateway threads wait on transfer service
6. Gateway thread pool fills
7. ENTIRE SYSTEM frozen from one slow dependency
```

### How do retries amplify the failure?

If three layers each retry three times, one user request can become twenty-seven downstream attempts. Under healthy conditions retries are invisible. Under degradation they become the dominant traffic source.

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

public class RetryAmplification {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int gatewayRetries = 3;
        int transferRetries = 3;
        int ledgerRetries = 3;

        int totalAttempts = gatewayRetries * transferRetries * ledgerRetries;
        System.out.println("Gateway retries: " + gatewayRetries);
        System.out.println("Transfer retries: " + transferRetries);
        System.out.println("Ledger retries: " + ledgerRetries);
        System.out.println("Total attempts per request: " + totalAttempts + " (3 x 3 x 3)");
        System.out.println("Under degradation: 100 user requests → " + (100 * totalAttempts)
                + " ledger hits");
    }
}
```

```text
Gateway retries: 3
Transfer retries: 3
Ledger retries: 3
Total attempts per request: 27 (3 x 3 x 3)
Under degradation: 100 user requests → 2700 ledger hits
```

### How do shared pools amplify the failure?

If all outbound calls share one thread pool, a single slow dependency consumes every thread, leaving none for calls to healthy dependencies.

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

public class SharedPoolProblem {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int poolSize = 20;
        int threadsStuckOnFraud = 18; // fraud-check is slow
        int threadsAvailableForBalance = poolSize - threadsStuckOnFraud;

        System.out.println("Shared thread pool size: " + poolSize);
        System.out.println("Threads stuck waiting on slow fraud-check: " + threadsStuckOnFraud);
        System.out.println("Threads left for balance lookups: " + threadsAvailableForBalance);
        System.out.println("Balance lookups (healthy!) start failing too");
        System.out.println("One slow dependency has starved ALL features");
    }
}
```

```text
Shared thread pool size: 20
Threads stuck waiting on slow fraud-check: 18
Threads left for balance lookups: 2
Balance lookups (healthy!) start failing too
One slow dependency has starved ALL features
```

### How does a bulkhead contain the failure?

A bulkhead gives each dependency its own thread pool, so a slow dependency exhausts only its own pool and healthy features keep their capacity.

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class BulkheadExample {

    static final ExecutorService fraudPool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(5);
    static final ExecutorService balancePool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(10);

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Fraud-check pool: 5 threads (isolated)");
        System.out.println("Balance pool: 10 threads (isolated)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("When fraud-check gets slow:");
        System.out.println("  Fraud pool: all 5 threads busy → fraud requests wait");
        System.out.println("  Balance pool: all 10 threads FREE → balance works perfectly");
        System.out.println("  Bulkhead prevents fraud from starving balance");

        fraudPool.shutdown();
        balancePool.shutdown();
    }
}
```

```text
Fraud-check pool: 5 threads (isolated)
Balance pool: 10 threads (isolated)

When fraud-check gets slow:
  Fraud pool: all 5 threads busy → fraud requests wait
  Balance pool: all 10 threads FREE → balance works perfectly
  Bulkhead prevents fraud from starving balance
```

### How do deadlines and retry budgets help?

Deadlines ensure slow calls are abandoned quickly. Retry budgets cap retries as a fraction of total traffic so they cannot multiply into a flood.

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

public class DeadlineAndBudget {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        long deadlineMs = 100;
        double retryBudgetPercent = 10.0;
        int totalRequests = 1000;
        int maxRetries = (int) (totalRequests * retryBudgetPercent / 100);

        System.out.println("Deadline: " + deadlineMs + "ms (abandon slow calls quickly)");
        System.out.println("Retry budget: " + retryBudgetPercent + "% of traffic");
        System.out.println("At " + totalRequests + " requests, max retries: " + maxRetries);
        System.out.println("Even if everything fails, retries cannot exceed " + maxRetries);
        System.out.println("Compare: without budget, 1000 requests x 3 retries = 3000 attempts");
    }
}
```

```text
Deadline: 100ms (abandon slow calls quickly)
Retry budget: 10.0% of traffic
At 1000 requests, max retries: 100
Even if everything fails, retries cannot exceed 100
Compare: without budget, 1000 requests x 3 retries = 3000 attempts
```

### What is graceful degradation?

Graceful degradation turns off non-essential features under stress so core functions keep working on reduced load.

```java
package com.bank.resilience;

public class GracefulDegradation {

    static boolean fraudCheckAvailable = false; // dependency down

    static String processTransfer(java.math.BigDecimal amount) {
        if (fraudCheckAvailable) {
            return "Fraud check passed, transfer approved";
        }
        // Degrade: approve small amounts, hold large for async review
        if (amount.compareTo(new java.math.BigDecimal("50000")) <= 0) {
            return "Approved (small amount, fraud check deferred)";
        }
        return "Held for async review (fraud check unavailable)";
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(processTransfer(new java.math.BigDecimal("10000")));
        System.out.println(processTransfer(new java.math.BigDecimal("500000")));
    }
}
```

```text
Approved (small amount, fraud check deferred)
Held for async review (fraud check unavailable)
```

### How do all the containment properties work together?

| Property | Breaks Which Amplifier | How |
|---|---|---|
| Bounded concurrency | Shared pools | Caps in-flight calls to a dependency |
| Deadlines | Synchronous chains | Abandons slow calls, frees threads |
| Retry budgets | Retry amplification | Caps retries as a fraction of traffic |
| Bulkheads | Shared pools | Isolates resources per dependency |
| Load shedding | All | Rejects excess at the edge |
| Backpressure | All | Signals "slow down" upstream |
| Graceful degradation | All | Drops non-essential features under stress |

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- How do you test for cascading failure before it happens in production?
- What is the difference between a circuit breaker and a bulkhead?
- Why is autoscaling sometimes counterproductive during a cascade?
- How do you design a system that degrades a little and recovers instead of collapsing?
